      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b0",
      "text": "TUR-DPO: Topology- and Uncertainty-Aware Direct Preference Optimization\n",
      "bbox": [
        149.03,
        47.23,
        447.56,
        56.19
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        596,
        188,
        1790,
        224
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 8.97,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 71,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b1",
      "text": "compute-constrained environments, robust to noisy or brittle\npairs, and easy to ablate or disable component-wise. The\nmethod is agnostic to the underlying transformer architec-\nture. It operates on tokenized responses and only requires\nlog probabilities from the policy and the reference. All ad-\nditional costs are confined to eliciting small graphs, running\na local verifier, and computing simple statistics such as vari-\nances and divergences. This makes the approach compatible\nwith existing DPO code paths and datasets without changing\nthe core infrastructure. The guiding principle is minimal-\nity: introduce just enough structure and uncertainty to steer\nthe optimization, but keep parameterization small so that\ntraining remains stable and reproducible. We empirically\nevaluate computational overhead, stability, and ablations for\neach component in Section 3 and Appendix.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.08,
        69.54,
        291.1,
        246.8
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        278,
        1164,
        987
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.86,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 880,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b2",
      "text": "Problem notation. For each prompt response pair (x, y),\nwe elicit a small directed graph G = (V, E) that captures\nthe internal reasoning. Nodes V are atomic subclaims or\nsteps, and edges E encode support or dependency relations.\nFrom (x, y, G) we compute three scalar signals. The se-\nmantic score ssem(x, y) summarizes task utility and faith-\nfulness. The topology score stopo(G) summarizes structural\nwell formedness. The uncertainty score u(G) aggregates\nepistemic dispersion across re elicited graphs and aleatoric\nambiguity in node correctness. These quantities feed a\ncompact shaped reward r\u03d5(x, y, G) and a per pair weight\nw \u2208(0, 1]. The reward alters the preference margin used\ninside a DPO style logistic loss, and the weight scales the\ncontribution of each pair to the objective. The notation fol-\nlows DPO conventions for policy and reference margins.\nWe write \u2206f \u2261f(x, y+) \u2212f(x, y\u2212) for brevity and use\n\u03c3(z) = 1/(1 + exp(\u2212z)) for the logistic function. The\nmethod is intentionally modular. If a dataset lacks reliable\ngraph extraction, the topology branch can be disabled by\nsetting its coefficient to zero. If uncertainty signals are\nunavailable, the weight can be set to a constant. This modu-\nlarity allows a path from DPO to the TUR-DPO system.\n",
      "bbox": [
        54.97,
        255.65,
        291.18,
        516.9
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        219,
        1022,
        1164,
        2067
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 1260,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b3",
      "text": "2.1. Topology, semantic, and uncertainty signals\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        531.98,
        257.95,
        541.95
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        2127,
        1031,
        2167
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 48,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b4",
      "text": "Topology elicitation and score.\nGiven a response y, we\nextract a graph by decomposing the text into atomic state-\nments, linking supports, and sanitizing cycles and duplicates.\nThe score aggregates simple indicators that correlate with\ncoherent multi-step reasoning. Minimal valid path qpath, cov-\nerage measures how many nodes and edges participate in at\nleast one acyclic path from premises to the final claim (Fig-\nure 2). Cycles ccycle, capture circular justification. Dangling\nnodes ddangling, capture unsupported claims. A contradiction\nsignal qcontradict, summarizes local logical conflicts along\nedges. We combine these into a linear score that remains\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.13,
        550.5,
        291.19,
        680.26
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2201,
        1164,
        2721
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.03,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 660,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b5",
      "text": "numerically stable during training.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        69.47,
        445.24,
        79.43
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        277,
        1780,
        317
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 35,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p3_b6",
      "text": "stopo(G) = \u03b11qpath \u2212\u03b12ccycle \u2212\u03b13ddangling \u2212\u03b14qcontradict.\n",
      "bbox": [
        310.24,
        89.27,
        538.64,
        100.73
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1240,
        357,
